          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>The three cords of the brachial plexus are named lateral, posterior, and medial </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>The three cords of the brachial plexus are named lateral, posterior, and medial based on their relationship to which structure?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Subclavian artery</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The subclavian artery is proximal to the cords. It becomes the axillary artery at the first rib, and it is around this renamed vessel that the cords are positioned.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>First rib</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The first rib marks where the subclavian becomes the axillary artery, but the cords are named for their position around the artery, not the rib.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pectoralis minor muscle</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pectoralis minor divides the axillary artery into three parts but is not the reference structure for naming the cords.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Axillary artery</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The lateral cord lies lateral to the axillary artery, the medial cord lies medial, and the posterior cord lies posterior to it.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The three cords are named for their position around the axillary artery: lateral cord lies lateral, medial cord lies medial, and posterior cord lies posterior to it."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Right. The cords wrap around the axillary artery and take their names from their position relative to it — lateral, posterior, and medial."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
