          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A newborn delivered by breech extraction presents with a claw hand and absent gr</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A newborn delivered by breech extraction presents with a claw hand and absent grip strength. Horner's syndrome is also noted. Which nerve roots are most likely damaged?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>C5 and C6</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>C5-C6 injury (upper trunk) causes Erb's palsy — waiter's tip position with preserved hand grip. It does not produce claw hand or Horner's syndrome.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>C8 and T1</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. C8-T1 (lower trunk) injury causes Klumpke's paralysis. C8 supplies long flexors, T1 supplies intrinsic hand muscles (producing the claw), and T1 carries sympathetic fibres to the eye (causing Horner's syndrome).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>C7 alone</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>C7 forms the middle trunk. Its isolated injury would affect wrist and finger extension, not produce a claw hand with Horner's syndrome.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>C5, C6, C7, C8, and T1</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Complete brachial plexus injury would paralyse the entire upper limb. The preserved shoulder function here points to a selective lower trunk lesion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Klumpke's paralysis involves C8 and T1 (lower trunk). C8 supplies long finger flexors; T1 supplies intrinsic hand muscles — their loss produces the claw. T1 also carries sympathetic fibres, so its disruption causes Horner's syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Klumpke's paralysis (C8, T1) presents with claw hand, lost grip, and Horner's syndrome when T1 sympathetic fibres are disrupted. Contrast with Erb's (C5, C6): shoulder and elbow affected, hand spared."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
