          "label": "<p>A patient cannot initiate arm abduction but can complete the movement once the a</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient cannot initiate arm abduction but can complete the movement once the arm is passively lifted past 15 degrees. Which rotator cuff muscle is most likely torn?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Infraspinatus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Infraspinatus is the primary lateral (external) rotator of the arm. Its injury weakens lateral rotation but does not impair initiation of abduction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Subscapularis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Subscapularis is the medial (internal) rotator covering the anterior scapula. Its injury does not affect the initiation of abduction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Supraspinatus</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Supraspinatus initiates the first 15 degrees of abduction before the deltoid takes over. It is the most commonly torn rotator cuff muscle, producing the classic painful arc.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Teres minor</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Teres minor assists infraspinatus in lateral rotation. It plays no role in initiating abduction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Supraspinatus initiates the first 15 degrees of abduction. Once torn, the patient cannot begin the movement but can complete it if assisted past that range, since deltoid takes over beyond 15°."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Right. Supraspinatus initiates abduction (0–15°), then deltoid continues to 90°. Its tear produces a painful arc and inability to begin the movement — a classic clinical sign."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
