          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which anatomical relationship explains why the axillary nerve is at risk during </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which anatomical relationship explains why the axillary nerve is at risk during fractures of the surgical neck of the humerus?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>It runs in the spiral (radial) groove of the humeral shaft</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The spiral groove houses the radial nerve, not the axillary nerve. Radial nerve injury occurs with mid-shaft humeral fractures, not surgical neck fractures.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It wraps directly around the surgical neck of the humerus</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The axillary nerve (from the posterior cord, C5-C6) courses around the surgical neck just below the humeral head, placing it in direct contact with the bone at this fracture-prone site.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It passes between the two heads of the biceps muscle</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The musculocutaneous nerve pierces the coracobrachialis. The axillary nerve takes a different course, wrapping posteriorly around the surgical neck of the humerus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It is tethered at the cervicoaxillary canal by the axillary sheath</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The axillary sheath wraps the neurovascular bundle at the apex of the axilla. The axillary nerve is vulnerable more distally, where it wraps around the surgical neck of the humerus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
